@meta
id: 24-18
title: The command-line window
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: cmdline-window
par: 48
minutes: 6
vscode: nothing like it
open: errors.ts

@brief
Two error maps, `clientErrors` on lines 2 to 7 and `serverErrors` on
lines 11 to 16, each need sorting by status code. You know the command
from lesson 14. Run it on the first block.

Now the second block needs the same command with a different range,
and the range is at the *front*. `Ctrl-B` gets you there but the
command line has no way to delete forward without arrow keys. So open
the command-line window instead: `q:`. It is a real buffer containing
your command history, one command per line, with an empty line at the
bottom for a new one. Every normal-mode key you own works in it. Go up
to the last command, change the range with `c` and a motion, and press
Enter on the line to run it.

@keys
| `q:` | open the command-line window |
| `k` | up onto the last command you ran |
| `cts` | change up to the `s` of `sort` — that is, the range |
| `Enter` | in the window: run the command under the cursor |
| `:q` | in the window: close it without running anything |

@hints
The first sort is `:2,7sort n /statusCode/`. Run it, look at the result.
--
`q:` opens the window with the cursor on the empty bottom line. `k` is
the command you just ran. The range is the first three characters, and
`cts` changes exactly up to the `s` of `sort`.
--
`q:` `k` `0cts11,16` `Esc` Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :2,7sort n /statusCode/<Enter>
    q: k 0 cts 11,16 <Esc> <Enter>
    :wq<Enter>

Forty-two keystrokes. Retyping the second command would have been
fifty-four, and over par.

`q:` is the answer to every command-line editing limitation at once.
History is a buffer, so `/statusCode` finds the command you want, `j`
and `k` walk through it, `ciw` and `ct/` and `f,` and `r` all do what
they always do, and Enter runs the line you are on. If you press `q:`
by accident, which everyone does when reaching for `:q`, `:q` closes
it. `q/` is the same window for search history, and `Ctrl-F` opens it
from a command line you are already halfway through typing, which is
the move when you realise mid-command that the fix is behind you.

The window is also a place to compose a command that is too long to
get right in one go. Type it, edit it as text, run it, and if it was
wrong, `q:` again and fix what was wrong rather than starting over.

@start errors.ts
export const clientErrors = {
  ORDER_NOT_FOUND: { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },
  TIER_3_REQUIRED: { statusCode: 403, sentry: false },
  ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED: { statusCode: 409, sentry: false },
  CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND: { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },
  PAYMENT_DECLINED: { statusCode: 402, sentry: false },
  REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED: { statusCode: 422, sentry: false },
};

export const serverErrors = {
  GATEWAY_V2_TIMEOUT: { statusCode: 504, sentry: true },
  EMAIL_DELIVERY_FAILED: { statusCode: 502, sentry: true },
  REFUND_FAILED: { statusCode: 502, sentry: true },
  DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE: { statusCode: 503, sentry: true },
  UNEXPECTED: { statusCode: 500, sentry: true },
  GATEWAY_V2_REJECTED: { statusCode: 502, sentry: true },
};

